 `.github/workflows/semgrep.yml` runs the open-source Semgrep scanner (its own pinned, hash-checked install in `requirements-semgrep.txt`; no account and no token; usage reporting is switched off, see the network paragraph below) on every pull request and every push to main. It reads the rule packs `p/python` and `p/javascript` from the public Semgrep registry over `src`, `scripts`, `load_tools`, `tools` and `n8n`, and fails the job on a finding of severity ERROR. After the install the job runs `pip check` and `semgrep --version`, so that a package missing from the lock file is reported as an install problem and not as a finding. The findings are printed in the job log only (no code-scanning upload). It belongs with the checks of `lint.yml` and the tests, and it needs the five folders above (a missing folder makes Semgrep exit 2, which the job tries up to three times in all before it fails with a message naming the possible causes). **Network use of the job:** pip downloads the packages of the lock file from the Python Package Index file hosts (every file is checked against its hash); `actions/setup-python` may download a CPython 3.12 build (GitHub-hosted releases of Python builds) when the runner does not already have it; Semgrep downloads the two rule packs from the Semgrep rule download service (a host name that ends in `.dev`) at every run; checkout reads this repository from GitHub. The workflow file names no other host (read from the file; no network capture was made, so this is not measured), and it sets `--metrics=off` and `--disable-version-check` to switch off Semgrep's usage report and version check. Tests in `tests/test_nosemgrep_form.py` run the real scan step against a fake `semgrep` script (only exit 2 is retried) and compare the parsed workflow with an exact whitelist: the triggers, the permissions, the concurrency block, one job on `ubuntu-24.04` with a time limit of at most 20 minutes, exactly five steps with exactly the expected keys (no `if`, `shell`, `working-directory`, `continue-on-error`, extra `env` or extra action), the two Actions pinned to the same commit ids as `lint.yml`, the install command word for word, and the scan command word for word (so a new flag, a `--config=...` or `--exclude=...` form, another pack or a missing folder fails). They also refuse an expression such as `github.event.`, `github.head_ref` or `github.ref_name` inside a `run:` step and any secret or token reference. These tests do not prove the workflow is safe or that it scans correctly on GitHub; they pin its shape and the retry logic (against a fake `semgrep` script).
 
-**Status of the scan:** no result of a run of this workflow is recorded in this file. **A green job alone does not prove that rules ran:** a job can end green with no rules loaded or no files read. The proof is the log line "Ran N rules on M files": after any change to the packs or the folders, read it and check that N and M are not zero and are plausible. This workflow has no check of its own for those numbers. `tests/` and `demo_only/` are not scanned, and findings of lower severity were not looked at. The rules are fetched live, so a later run can give other numbers. If a run fails on a finding that already exists, fix the code or suppress that one finding as described below in a separate change.
